# Claude Code — User-level instructions

## Communication style

- Be terse. No preamble, no trailing "here's what I did" summaries — I can read the diff.
- No emojis unless I ask.
- When referencing code, include `file:line` so I can jump to it.
- If something is ambiguous, ask one focused question rather than listing all possibilities.

## Config & dotfiles management (chezmoi)

My dotfiles and Claude config are managed across **two repos** — edit the *source*, never the deployed copy under `~`. A hand-edit to a deployed file is reverted on the next `chezmoi apply` (templated sources) or silently drifts. Run `chezmoi source-path <file>` before editing any config under `~`; a `.tmpl` source means the deployed file is generated and hand-edits there are pointless.

- **Personal / general** → chezmoi, source `~/.local/share/chezmoi` (remote `DanielH2018/dotfiles`, `.chezmoiroot=home`). Owns most of `~/.claude` as **real files** under `home/private_dot_claude/`. `~/.claude/settings.json` is **generated** from `home/.chezmoitemplates/settings.base.json` — to change settings/hooks/permissions, edit that base template, then `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/settings.json`.
- **Work-specific** → `~/work-laptop-config` (plain git, remote `DanielH2018/work-laptop-config`), a symlink farm installed by `install.sh`. Owns `CLAUDE.local.md`, `docs/{integrations,enforcement}.md`, work agents, and PCI/redaction hooks — these appear as **symlinks** in `~/.claude`.

Committing in each repo stays manual: `~/.local/share/chezmoi` and `~/work-laptop-config` respectively.

## Git conventions
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" }}

- Commits are signed via 1Password SSH agent — never pass `--no-verify` or any flag that bypasses signing.
{{- end }}
- Default branch is `main`. Prefer rebase over merge.
- Always create a new commit rather than amending unless I explicitly ask to amend.
- Write commit messages that explain *why*, not just what changed. Example:
  ```
  Fix race condition in settlement processor on concurrent retries

  The previous implementation didn't hold the lock across the
  DB read + write, allowing duplicate settlements under load.
  ```

## GitHub

- For anything on `github.com` — PRs, issues, diffs, Actions runs/logs, repo files, releases — use the `gh` CLI (`gh pr view`, `gh issue view`, `gh api`, `gh run view --log`), not WebFetch. `gh` is authenticated, sees private repos, and returns structured data. `WebFetch(domain:github.com)` is denied in settings, so fetching it will fail — reach for `gh` first. (`raw.githubusercontent.com` stays fetchable for raw files `gh` doesn't cover cleanly.)

## Testing

Write tests for any new code I create. Match the style and framework already used in the project.

## What I don't want

- Don't add docstrings, comments, or type annotations to code you didn't change.
- Don't refactor or "clean up" code beyond what the task requires.
- Don't add error handling for scenarios that can't happen.
- Don't create new files when editing an existing one would do.

## Source quality & epistemic honesty

Applies to every session — answers, summaries, research, and anything written to a durable store:
- Separate established fact from speculation, prediction, or opinion; don't present hedged or future-tense claims as settled.
- Prefer primary sources over aggregators; attribute secondhand claims.
- On conflicting sources, favor recency + consistency and flag the discrepancy rather than silently picking one.
- Never assert anything about code, files, or output you haven't actually read; say so when it's undetermined.

## Specs & implementation plans

- When you produce an implementation plan or a spec for my review — in plan mode or in normal chat — also render it as an HTML artifact for readability, in addition to the written plan, not instead of it. Load the `artifact-design` skill first.
- Keep it **local by default**: write a self-contained HTML file to `~/.claude/artifacts/` (create the dir if needed) and tell me the path. Do **not** publish to claude.ai or call the Artifact tool unless I explicitly ask you to send it there.
- Skip the artifact only when the plan is trivial (a one-liner or single obvious step) or I've said not to.

## Compaction policy

When compacting, always preserve:
- The full list of files modified in this session
- All test commands run and their pass/fail results
- Any user corrections or architectural decisions
- Current task state and next steps
- Active branch name and whether changes are pushed

